```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment  = "#>",
  warning  = FALSE,
  message  = FALSE,
  fig.path = "nb-convergence-figs/"
)
```

Negative-binomial regression estimates both the response mean and theta, which controls variation beyond a Poisson distribution. The fit alternates between updating coefficients with theta fixed and updating theta with the fitted means fixed. Coefficient updates use iteratively reweighted least squares (IRLS), a sequence of weighted regressions; theta updates maximize the likelihood with respect to that single parameter.
Both `MASS::glm.nb()` and `fastglm_nb()` follow this strategy, but their
implementations differ in initialization and numerical handling.  This
vignette compares the two on a deliberately challenging data-generating
process where MASS frequently fails to converge.

## Data-generating process

We use a moderately high-dimensional negative-binomial model
($n = 200$, $p = 9$) with strong alternating effects and true
$\theta = 1$.
The large coefficients produce fitted means spanning many orders of
magnitude, which stresses the IRLS solver when the initial $\theta$
estimate is far from the truth.

```{r dgp}
library(fastglm)
library(MASS)

n <- 200
p <- 9
beta_true  <- c(4, 1.5, -1.2, 1.5, -1.2, 1.5, -1.2, 1.5, -1.2)
theta_true <- 1
n_reps     <- 200
```

## Convergence comparison

For each of `r n_reps` replications we draw a fresh design matrix and
response, then fit with both `fastglm_nb()` and `MASS::glm.nb()`.  We
record whether each method converges without error or warning, and when
both converge, we compare the maximized log-likelihoods.

```{r comparison, cache = TRUE}
results <- data.frame(
    seed         = integer(n_reps),
    mass_conv    = logical(n_reps),
    fg_conv      = logical(n_reps),
    mass_loglik  = numeric(n_reps),
    fg_loglik    = numeric(n_reps),
    mass_theta   = numeric(n_reps),
    fg_theta     = numeric(n_reps)
)

for (i in seq_len(n_reps)) {
    set.seed(i)
    X  <- cbind(1, matrix(rnorm(n * (p - 1)), n, p - 1))
    mu <- exp(X %*% beta_true)
    y  <- MASS::rnegbin(n, mu = mu, theta = theta_true)

    ## MASS::glm.nb
    mass_ok   <- FALSE
    mass_ll   <- NA_real_
    mass_th   <- NA_real_
    tryCatch(
        withCallingHandlers(
            {
                m <- MASS::glm.nb(y ~ X[, -1])
                if (m$converged) {
                    mass_ok <- TRUE
                    mass_ll <- as.numeric(logLik(m))
                    mass_th <- m$theta
                }
            },
            warning = function(w) invokeRestart("muffleWarning")
        ),
        error = function(e) NULL
    )

    ## fastglm_nb
    fg_ok  <- FALSE
    fg_ll  <- NA_real_
    fg_th  <- NA_real_
    tryCatch({
        f <- fastglm_nb(X, y, link = "log")
        if (f$converged && !any(is.nan(coef(f)))) {
            fg_ok <- TRUE
            fg_ll <- as.numeric(logLik(f))
            fg_th <- f$theta
        }
    }, error = function(e) NULL)

    results$seed[i]        <- i
    results$mass_conv[i]   <- mass_ok
    results$fg_conv[i]     <- fg_ok
    results$mass_loglik[i] <- mass_ll
    results$fg_loglik[i]   <- fg_ll
    results$mass_theta[i]  <- mass_th
    results$fg_theta[i]    <- fg_th
}
```

### Summary

```{r summary-table}
tab <- with(results, data.frame(
    Method     = c("MASS::glm.nb", "fastglm_nb"),
    Converged  = c(sum(mass_conv), sum(fg_conv)),
    Failed     = c(sum(!mass_conv), sum(!fg_conv)),
    Rate       = sprintf("%.1f%%", 100 * c(mean(mass_conv), mean(fg_conv)))
))
knitr::kable(tab, align = "lrrl",
             caption = sprintf("Convergence over %d replications", n_reps))
```

`fastglm_nb()` converges on `r sum(results$fg_conv)` of `r n_reps` replications, and
`MASS::glm.nb()` fails on `r sum(!results$mass_conv)`.

### Log-likelihood comparison

When both methods converge they should find the same MLE, so their
log-likelihoods should agree.  In a small number of replications
`MASS::glm.nb()` reports convergence with an extreme theta estimate
with $\hat\theta > 10^6$ and very poor log-likelihood.  We exclude
these (identified by `fastglm` achieving a strictly higher
log-likelihood by more than 1 unit) from the following agreement plot. These exclusions mean that the plot describes only the remaining subset, and the excluded fits must still be counted when evaluating numerical reliability:

```{r loglik-comparison}
both <- results$mass_conv & results$fg_conv
ll_diff <- results$fg_loglik[both] - results$mass_loglik[both]

n_degenerate <- sum(ll_diff > 1)
agree <- both
agree[both] <- ll_diff <= 1
ll_good <- results$fg_loglik[agree] - results$mass_loglik[agree]
```

Both methods converge on `r sum(both)` of `r n_reps` replications. `MASS::glm.nb()` reaches a degenerate fit in `r n_degenerate` of these, which leaves `r sum(agree)` agreeing fits. Among them, the largest absolute log-likelihood difference is `r formatC(max(abs(ll_good)), format = "e", digits = 1)`, and the mean is `r formatC(mean(abs(ll_good)), format = "e", digits = 1)`.

```{r loglik-plot, fig.width = 6, fig.height = 4.5}
library(ggplot2)
agreement <- results[agree, ]
ggplot(agreement, aes(mass_loglik, fg_loglik)) +
    geom_abline(intercept = 0, slope = 1, colour = "grey40", linetype = 2) +
    geom_point(colour = "#0B5CAD", alpha = 0.5, size = 1.2) +
    labs(x = "MASS log-likelihood", y = "fastglm log-likelihood",
         title = "Log-likelihood agreement") +
    theme_bw(base_size = 11) + theme(panel.grid.minor = element_blank())
```

### Theta estimates

```{r theta-plot, fig.width = 6, fig.height = 4.5}
ggplot(agreement, aes(mass_theta, fg_theta)) +
    geom_abline(intercept = 0, slope = 1, colour = "grey40", linetype = 2) +
    geom_point(colour = "#0B5CAD", alpha = 0.5, size = 1.2) +
    labs(x = expression(hat(theta) ~ "(MASS)"), y = expression(hat(theta) ~ "(fastglm)"),
         title = expression("Dispersion" ~ hat(theta) ~ "agreement")) +
    theme_bw(base_size = 11) + theme(panel.grid.minor = element_blank())
```

The largest absolute theta difference among these fits is `r formatC(max(abs(results$fg_theta[agree] - results$mass_theta[agree])), format = "e", digits = 1)`.

### Cases where only fastglm converges

```{r fg-only}
fg_only <- results$fg_conv & !results$mass_conv
```

`fastglm_nb()` converges while `MASS::glm.nb()` fails in `r sum(fg_only)` replications. Across these fits, theta ranges from `r round(min(results$fg_theta[fg_only]), 3)` to `r round(max(results$fg_theta[fg_only]), 3)`, and the log likelihood ranges from `r round(min(results$fg_loglik[fg_only]), 1)` to `r round(max(results$fg_loglik[fg_only]), 1)`.
